Hello everybody,
Finally I found a recent IASL binary which seems to work. However, the=20
use of this customized DSDT is not satisfactory. First, the FAN is now=20
always on. Second, when the battery is fully loaded, the battery level=20
is reported at 9% of remaining capacity. I own an ACER Travelmate=20
4001Lmi, so I tried the patch dedicated to this model, but the result is=20
not correct. The only positive point is that the bug addressed with the=20
patch acpi-ec-nospinlock doens't seem to occur. There is no loss of=20
reactivity when I use the SBS-CM method to access the battery. I have=20
put in the attachement the original an pacthed DSDT table.

Thanks for your help,
Fran=E7ois Valenduc

Rich Townsend a =E9crit :

> Hi all --
>
> I've finished the first cut of the DSDT hackery needed to get smart=20
> battery systems (SBS) working like 'traditional' control method (CM)=20
> batteries. This sounds like a kludge, but it's worth pointing out that=20
> many modern laptops already use SBS batteries internally, but expose=20
> them as CM batteries.
>
> The new code can be downloaded from the new SourceForge project I've=20
> established:
>
> https://sourceforge.net/projects/sbs-linux/
>
> The package to download is SBS-CM-20050205. Basically, this package=20
> provides patches against the DSDT (in source form, as decompiled using=20
> the Intel compiler), that adds in the SBS-CM support. Full=20
> instructions are provided in the accompanying README file.
>
> The following systems are "supported":
>
>  *) Acer Extensa 3002WLMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4000LMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4001LMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4001WLMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4002LMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4502LMi
>  *) Acer TravelMate 4502WLMi
>  *) Acer Extensa 3202XCi
>
> I'm currently working on the Acer Aspire 1692WLMi, which already=20
> contains a broken SBS-CM implementation. The Acer TravelMate 382 and=20
> Ferrari 3400 also already contain SBS-CM implementations, but I'm not=20
> sure to what extent they work or not.
>
> As well as the DSDT patches, I provide in the package my embedded=20
> controller patch, that improves the EC responsiveness by switching=20
> from spinlocks to semaphores. I've tried out the other patches=20
> mentioned recently on this list, but they either don't work, or reduce=20
> EC access rates to a crawl.
>
> Note that battery events, including alarms, are properly handled; so=20
> you should get notifications in /proc/acpi/event when something=20
> interesting happens. In tandem with acpid, you can use these=20
> notifications to, e.g., shutdown your system when the battery gets low.
>
> Bug reports, feature requests, etc, should be submitted using the=20
> SourceForge project page -- not to the acpi-devel list, since it would=20
> be a bit rude to flood the list with this stuff. But of course,=20
> general smart battery discussion should continue on acpi-devel.
>
> Happy battery monitoring!
>
> cheers,
>
> Rich
